    {
      "id": "finding:pilgrimage:r10:1.7",
      "label": "Finding 1.7\nThe vulnerability occurs when…",
      "type": "Finding",
      "color": "#06D6A0",
      "status": null,
      "title": "The vulnerability occurs when parsing PNG files, which may contain embedded references to other files. A malicious actor could craft a PNG or use an existing one and add a textual chunk type (e.g., tEXt) with the keyword \"profile\" that ImageMagick interprets as a filename and loads the content as a raw profile. Exploitation path involves crafting a malicious PNG file with a tEXt chunk containing a profile attribute referencing a local file. When ImageMagick is used to process the image, the contents of the referenced files are embedded into the new image. A Proof of Concept (PoC) was tested using a simple Rust application from a GitHub repository. The PoC creates the malicious PNG. The PoC was used to embed the contents of the /etc/passwd file into a PNG image, exploiting CVE-2022-44268. The hex-encoded contents of the /etc/passwd file were successfully embedded inside the PNG as part of the exploit",
      "size": 32,
      "shape": "dot",
      "borderColor": "#212529",
      "borderWidth": 2,
      "font": {
        "color": "#212529"
      }
    },
